## 5. Impact on Historical Data

This applies both to a wording change (no new column, no impact at all) and to the related scenario of the source team adding a brand-new question in future (a new column) — addressed here for completeness since it will surface the same way.

### 5.1 Wording Change (the current request)

A wording change only updates the value inside an existing text column. No new column is introduced, no schema changes, and historical Parquet files are completely unaffected.

### 5.2 New Question Added 
Parquet files already written to S3 are immutable — a new column added going forward does not rewrite historical files. This creates a mix of old files (without the new column) and new files (with it) in the same S3 path, split by date.

- The Glue Crawler detects the new column from the newer files and automatically adds it to the Glue Data Catalog table — no manual Catalog edit is needed.
- When queried through Starburst, older rows that predate the new column automatically return `NULL` for that column. This is standard, correct behavior for Parquet plus schema-on-read — not a defect and not something we need to build.
- Aggregate functions (`AVG`, `SUM`, `COUNT`) already skip `NULL`s by default, so YTD/daily aggregates continue to work without additional handling.

**Important:** this NULL-backfill behavior is identical whether we keep five survey-specific tables or move to one unified table. A unified table does not avoid this — if anything, it is worse, because a question asked only on one survey type would show as permanently `NULL` for every other survey type's rows, forever, not just for historical dates. The current per-survey design keeps each new column scoped only to the table where it is actually relevant.

---

## 6. Side-by-Side Comparison

| Aspect | Current Architecture (minor fix) | Proposed Unified Table |
|---|---|---|
| Change needed for a wording update | Edit 1 config value (or 2 code lines today) | Same edit still required — a source system wording change must still be mapped somewhere |
| Code/pipeline impact | No schema change, no table redesign | Full redesign of curation + sentiment scripts, all writes, all reads |
| Historical data | Untouched, fully compatible | Requires backfill/migration of all existing curated + sentiment history |
| New question added by source (e.g. NPS) | Adds 1 column to the one relevant survey table; other tables unaffected | Adds 1 column to the shared table; permanently NULL for every survey type that never asks it |
| Downstream BI (Tableau) | No changes to existing dashboards/queries | Every dashboard, Athena view, and Starburst query needs to be rebuilt against the new shared schema |
| Risk | Low — isolated, incremental | High — single shared table becomes a bottleneck; one bad load can affect all survey types at once |

